This API wrapper automatically uses Pex instrumentation methods to generate and execute parameterized unit tests. For example, instead of a traditional test method that takes and returns a single argument, you'll see a method that takes an array of inputs where the first value is the method to execute (for example: Add, Remove, or Insert) and the remaining values depend on the method's parameters.

Keep in mind that these are Pex-generated tests and should be treated as examples of how Pex might generate your methods. They require you to have a baseline understanding of your code and how Pex works to utilize the results.

## Generating tests with Pex ##
Pex creates test methods for the public methods in your classes. By using Pex's default settings, Pex will generate up to 100 test cases for each method. 

It will use parameters passed into your methods, including both valid and invalid parameters. It will also try to include edge cases to ensure your method behaves as expected in all scenarios.

If a method call throws an exception, Pex will try to create parameters that cause the exception to be thrown, regardless of whether this makes sense in the context of the method's behaviour. (For example, trying to add a duplicate item to a has set.)

Note that Pex may not be able to generate test cases for constructors and most non-void methods (since you can't call void methods with arguments, and constructors are invoked implicitly, not explicitly).

## Using Pex with your classes ##

To create tests with Pex, you need to run the Pex tool against your code. By adding the `PexClass` attribute to your test class, Pex will generate tests for your class's members. 

Here is a sample of how it would work:

1. You write the code of your class, with public methods like `Add`, `Remove`, `Insert`, etc.
2. You add `[PexClass]` attribute to the test class.
3. You add `[PexMethod]`--------------------            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the last position of x in old array is greater than some position of x in new array
