}public class Solution {
    public int Calculate(string s) {
        int result=0, sign=1, num=0;
        Stack<Integer> stack= new Stack<>();
        for(int i=0;i<s.length();i++){
            char ch = s.charAt(i);
            if(Character.isDigit(ch)){
                num = num*10 + (ch - '0');
            }else{
                result += sign*num;
                num = 0;
                if(ch=='+'){
                    sign = 1;
                }else if(ch=='-'){
                    sign = -1;
                }else if(ch=='('){
                    stack.push(result);
                    stack.push(sign);
                    result = 0;
                    sign = 1;
                }else if(ch==')'){
                    int prev_sign = stack.pop();
                    int prev_result = stack.pop();
                    result = prev_result + prev_sign*result;
                }
            }
        }
        if(num != 0) result += sign*num;
        return result;
    }
}Approach: Use a stack to deal with parentheses. For every character in the string: 1. if it is a digit, form the entire number. 2. If it is a + or -, save the sign before it. 3. If it is a '(', push the result and sign so far to stack, and restart calculation. 4. If it is a ')', pop the top two items from stack treating them as the result and sign outside the parentheses level. Combine them with result inside parentheses. This is due to the properties of BODMAS rule which states that parentheses can change the precedence of operations. Till now, we've done calculation for every character including the last one. Add remaining number (if any) to the result.Algorithm Time complexity: O(n) every character is pushed and popped only once. Space Complexity: O(n) in worst case scenario (only when all characters are '(' or ')' ). In that case, stack will save the results of all subexpressions.Algorithmic complexity (Time & Space) = O(n) because of the single pass through the string without any nested loops. It is optimized through the usage of Stack data structure to handle nested parentheses. The usage of Stack data structure ensures the order of computation, which is in alignment with the rules governing the order of operations (BODMAS/PEMDAS). Also, it keeps the space complexity to a minimum as no additional data structures are used beyond a stack in this solution. This solution is therefore both time and space efficient. This solution is optimized for the 'Calculate' Problem as it takes into account the potential for nested parentheses.
​
In this problem, the string s can include digits, '+', '-', '(', and ')' and mathematical operations follow the rules of BODMAS, which is a subset of PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division / Operations, Addition and Subtraction). The goal is to compute the result by following these rules. This process can also be explained as parsing a mathematical equation. As such, using a stack is a good strategy to manage calculations at different levels of parentheses due to the way they operate. It is also useful to have the current sum and the current sign (either + or -) when you get to the innermost parentheses.--------------------        }

